<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      `enableOnBackInvokedCallback` is the app-wide opt-in to predictive back. The shell already answers back with a
      `BackHandler` (an open side surface closes to HOME); without this the system runs the legacy path and the
      predictive animation never plays.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".LauncherApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:largeHeap="true"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.MorphicLauncher2">

        <!--
          **This is the flip that makes the APK a launcher** (P9 in the rewrite plan): `category.HOME` is what puts
          Morphic in the system's home-app chooser, and `category.DEFAULT` is what lets an implicit HOME intent — the
          home button — resolve to it at all. Everything above this line has been a launcher-shaped app.

          Four attributes come with the role, each answering something the home button does that an ordinary activity
          never faces:

          - `launchMode="singleTask"` — pressing home while already home must *return* to the running instance, not
            stack a second one on top of it.
          - `stateNotNeeded="true"` — the system may kill and restart a home app freely, and it must come back
            without a saved `Bundle`. The launcher's state is its stores, not its instance state, so this is a
            statement of fact rather than a concession.
          - `resumeWhilePausing="true"` — home is allowed to resume while the app being left is still pausing, which
            is what makes the home button feel immediate rather than waiting on whatever is on screen.
          - `configChanges="…"` — the launcher handles every configuration change itself. Compose re-reads the window
            and recomposes; recreating the activity on a rotate would tear down the drag, the open folder and every
            baked icon for no gain.

          **One filter, where L1 had two.** L1 declared a second `MAIN` + `LAUNCHER` filter beside this one, which is
          redundant: an intent matches a filter when the action matches and *every* category on the intent is present
          on the filter, so a plain MAIN/LAUNCHER query already matches this filter's superset. `LAUNCHER` stays here
          so the launcher is reachable from its own app list like any other app.

          **`windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"`, and the point of it is that it stops the system panning.** The
          attribute was absent while nothing on a launcher surface took text; the APPS search field asks for a
          keyboard now, and an absent attribute means `adjustUnspecified`, which the system resolved to *pan* — it
          slid the whole window up by the keyboard's height, taking the top row of icons off the screen and the
          status bar with it. It also broke the frost, and not visibly-as-a-consequence: `wallpaperBackdrop` samples
          the wallpaper by **screen** position, so a window panned up by 500px asks for a crop 500px above the
          picture, and `drawBlurredCrop` clamps to the bitmap and leaves that band of the film empty — a hole
          through to the sharp wallpaper across the top half of the surface.

          `adjustResize` on an edge-to-edge window (API 30+, which `enableEdgeToEdge` puts us on) resizes nothing
          either: it makes the IME arrive as `WindowInsets.ime`, for **each surface** to answer. Deliberately not
          answered once around the whole nav host, which is the obvious thing and is wrong here: "does the keyboard
          resize me?" is a real per-surface question, and a launcher grid's answer is no. A page of the APPS pager
          is `rows × cols` divided out of its box, so shrinking the box shrinks the cells and the icons in them —
          the arrangement cramps rather than being covered. A sheet, a picker or the icon studio wants the opposite.
          The IME still stays out of `uiInsets`, which is a different question: that is what a surface reserves
          *permanently*.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|uiMode|density"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:resumeWhilePausing="true"
            android:stateNotNeeded="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
